@namespace YourApp.Components.Ui

@*
    TEMPLATE — rename the @namespace above to your own.

    A modal sheet: one self-contained task, and completing it is the only goal.

    THE DECISION RULE, which is the whole of when to be modal:

        Use a sheet when the task is self-contained and completing it is the only
        goal. Use a non-modal floating panel when the operator needs to see or
        compare other things while working. Comparison is never modal. If you are
        unsure, do not be modal.

    A modal tier is worth having beside a floating one, for the cases the floating layer
    serves badly: a form that must complete or cancel, a multi-step configuration. It does
    not replace the floating layer, and it must not swallow it — an app whose core workflow
    is comparing two things has no business making either of them modal.

    MudDialog underneath owns the scrim, the focus trap, and the stacking — plumbing that
    is genuinely hard to get right and which MudBlazor already has. Everything visible
    comes from .app-sheet.

    Where the width comes from, so it is not re-litigated: MudDialog owns its outer
    element, so the sheet cannot wrap itself in a sized container. The dialog's actual
    width is set by the caller through DialogOptions.MaxWidth; the Width parameter here
    applies min-width to the inner body so a sheet with little content still reads as a
    sheet rather than a tooltip.

    Class="app-sheet" is load-bearing, not decoration. It lands on Mud's own .mud-dialog
    element, and the surface rules are written as .mud-dialog.app-sheet so they out-specify
    both MudBlazor's own .mud-dialog and the bridge's — neither of which this sheet could
    beat on source order, since apple-bridge.css loads after the component stylesheet.
*@

<MudDialog Class="app-sheet">
    <TitleContent>
        <div class="app-sheet__head">
            <div class="app-sheet__titles">
                <span class="app-sheet__title">@Title</span>
                @if (Subtitle is not null)
                {
                    <span class="app-sheet__sub">@Subtitle</span>
                }
            </div>
            <button type="button"
                    class="app-sheet__close"
                    title="Close"
                    @onclick="Cancel">
                <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Close" />
            </button>
        </div>
    </TitleContent>

    <DialogContent>
        <div class="app-sheet__body" style="min-width:@Width">
            @ChildContent
        </div>
    </DialogContent>

    <DialogActions>
        @if (Footer is not null)
        {
            <div class="app-sheet__foot">
                @Footer
            </div>
        }
    </DialogActions>
</MudDialog>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] private IMudDialogInstance? Instance { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter] public string? Subtitle { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The task's own buttons. Exactly one of them should be Filled — the one that
    /// completes the task — and Cancel should be Plain beside it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Footer { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A floor for the body, not the dialog's width. The caller sets the real width through
    /// DialogOptions.MaxWidth; this only keeps a thin sheet from collapsing to a tooltip.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string Width { get; set; } = "520px";

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised when the operator leaves without completing. A sheet that cannot be
    /// cancelled is a trap.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Do not rely on this to know the outcome. Escape and a backdrop click go through
    /// MudBlazor's own cancel and never reach this handler — read the DialogResult at the
    /// call site instead. This callback is for cleanup the sheet itself owns.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnCancel { get; set; }

    private async Task Cancel()
    {
        await OnCancel.InvokeAsync();

        Instance?.Cancel();
    }
}
